{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. Each lottery has two outcomes, so the threshold for an outcome to be considered 'probable' is 1/2 = 50%. In Lottery A: -3000 Euros occurs with 25% probability (≤50%, not probable), and 0 Euros with 75% probability (>50%, probable). Thus, the only probable outcome in A is 0 Euros, so the average of probable outcomes is 0. In Lottery B: -4000 Euros occurs with 20% probability (≤50%, not probable), and 0 Euros with 80% probability (>50%, probable). The only probable outcome in B is also 0 Euros, so the average of probable outcomes is 0. Since both lotteries have the same average (0) over their probable outcomes, the decision reason is indifferent between A and B.",
